      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\) formed by two sectors of a circle with a radius of 12, each with an angle of \\(60^\\circ\\), placed side by side.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the area of one sector. The formula for the area of a sector of a circle is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of sector} = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi r^2\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) is the central angle in degrees and \\(r\\) is the radius of the circle.\n\nFor our sectors, \\(\\theta = 60^\\circ\\) and \\(r = 12\\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi \\times 12^2\n\\]\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\pi \\times 144\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{144\\pi}{6}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one sector} = 24\\pi\n\\]\n\nSince there are two sectors, the total area of the figure \\(ABCD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total area} = 2 \\times 24\\pi\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Total area} = 48\\pi\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is \\(\\boxed{48\\pi}\\).",
